BE=ref('NULOJIX (belatacept): Prescribing Information','https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=c16ac648-d5d2-9f7d-8637-e2328572754e')
q('Why is mycophenolate generally replaced before a planned pregnancy in a transplant recipient?', 'It is associated with pregnancy loss and congenital malformations',[
 ('All immunosuppression should be stopped before conception','This risks rejection and graft loss.'),('Mycophenolate is the preferred pregnancy-safe antimetabolite','It is teratogenic.'),('Pregnancy planning has no effect on transplant drug selection','A planned regimen review is essential.')],
 'Preserve the graft while selecting a pregnancy-compatible regimen', '**Mycophenolate requires preconception discontinuation under specialist supervision.**',['Azathioprine is commonly considered as an alternative when appropriate.','Stable graft function, blood pressure and the whole regimen need review.'], '**Plan the switch before attempting conception.**',['Do not simply stop all agents.','Use coordinated transplant and maternal-medicine counseling and current product-label precautions.'],[['Mycophenolate','Teratogenic risk'],['Alternative','Often azathioprine when suitable'],['Goal','Maternal, fetal and graft safety']],[TR,EA])
q('Which action explains mycophenolate’s immunosuppressive effect?', 'Inhibition of inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase and de novo guanine nucleotide synthesis',[
 ('Calcineurin inhibition','That is the principal mechanism of tacrolimus and cyclosporine.'),('Direct mTOR inhibition','That describes sirolimus or everolimus.'),('CD28 costimulation blockade through CD80/86 binding','That describes belatacept.')],
 'Lymphocyte nucleotide synthesis is a therapeutic target', '**Activated lymphocytes rely strongly on de novo purine synthesis.**',['Gastrointestinal intolerance and cytopenias can limit treatment.','Infection risk reflects the combined immunosuppressive burden.'], '**Monitor the regimen rather than one drug in isolation.**',['Review blood counts and symptoms.','Dose changes should consider rejection risk and concurrent infection.'],[['Mycophenolate','IMPDH inhibition'],['CNI','Calcineurin blockade'],['mTOR inhibitor','Downstream growth signaling']],[TR,EA])
q('Which recipient characteristic is required for belatacept use because of PTLD risk?', 'Established EBV seropositivity',[
 ('EBV seronegativity','Seronegative recipients are at particularly increased PTLD risk.'),('Unknown EBV status without testing','The label restricts use when status is unknown.'),('ABO group O irrespective of EBV','ABO group does not replace the EBV requirement.')],
 'A targeted costimulation blocker has a specific safety restriction', '**Belatacept binds CD80/86 and blocks CD28-mediated T-cell costimulation.**',['PTLD can involve the CNS.','The total immunosuppressive regimen affects infection and malignancy risk.'], '**Confirm eligibility and monitor under a transplant protocol.**',['Do not extrapolate the label to unapproved organ settings.','New neurological or systemic symptoms need assessment.'],[['Mechanism','Costimulation blockade'],['Required status','EBV seropositive'],['Major warning','PTLD, including CNS disease']],[BE])
q('Proteinuria, dyslipidemia, oral ulcers and impaired wound healing arise after sirolimus initiation. Which drug class is implicated?', 'mTOR inhibitors',[
 ('Calcineurin inhibitors as the specific class for this cluster','They have a different characteristic toxicity profile, although effects can overlap.'),('Antiviral polymerase inhibitors','These are not sirolimus’s class.'),('Mineralocorticoid receptor agonists','This is unrelated to the drug’s mechanism.')],
 'Avoid assuming every CNI-sparing strategy is toxicity free', '**mTOR inhibition alters lymphocyte proliferation and tissue repair pathways.**',['Proteinuria and delayed wound healing affect suitability.','Pneumonitis and cytopenias are additional concerns.'], '**Review timing and the full risk-benefit balance.**',['Conversion is individualized rather than universal.','Monitor urine protein, lipids, counts and clinical symptoms.'],[['Class','Sirolimus/everolimus'],['Toxicity','Wound healing and metabolic effects'],['Kidney issue','Proteinuria can worsen']],[TR,EA])
q('A kidney recipient has BK DNAemia of 4,000 copies/mL on two samples three weeks apart. Which strategy is central to the 2024 consensus guidance?', 'Stepwise reduction of immunosuppression with close viral and graft monitoring',[
 ('Automatically intensify immunosuppression as if every creatinine rise were rejection','This can worsen viral replication.'),('Treat routinely with leflunomide or cidofovir as proven first-line therapy','The consensus does not support these as routine effective treatments.'),('Ignore persistent DNAemia until systemic symptoms appear','BK injury can be clinically silent before graft dysfunction.')],
 'BK control usually requires rebalancing immunosuppression', '**Persistent DNAemia above 1,000 copies/mL or values above 10,000 warrant action in the guideline framework.**',['Assay-equivalent thresholds and serial trends matter.','Reduction strategies target antimetabolite, CNI or both according to protocol.'], '**Balance viral clearance against rejection risk.**',['Follow plasma viral load and graft function.','Biopsy depends on dysfunction, immune risk and the clinical context.'],[['Persistent intermediate DNAemia','Confirm then reduce burden'],['High load or nephropathy','Act promptly'],['Monitoring','Virus and graft/rejection risk']],[BK],'clinical_scenario')
q('Tubulitis is present in a graft biopsy and viral nuclear staining supports polyomavirus infection. Why is this distinction important?', 'BK nephropathy can mimic cellular rejection, but usually calls for reducing rather than escalating immunosuppression',[
 ('Tubulitis makes viral injury impossible','The patterns can overlap.'),('Positive viral staining proves there can never be concurrent rejection','Both processes can occur and require integrated assessment.'),('BK is treated by routine pulse steroids alone','That can aggravate replication.')],
 'A similar lesion can require an opposite treatment direction', '**Viral cytopathic change and SV40-related staining support polyomavirus nephropathy in context.**',['Plasma DNAemia provides additional evidence.','Not every inflammatory focus can be confidently assigned to rejection during viral injury.'], '**Integrate pathology, virology and immunological risk.**',['Use a specialist plan for concurrent suspected processes.','Monitor after immunosuppression changes.'],[['BK injury','Viral replication'],['Cellular rejection','Alloimmune injury'],['Treatment balance','Requires integrated evidence']],[BK,BA])
q('Which donor-recipient CMV serostatus combination usually confers the highest primary CMV risk after kidney transplantation?', 'Donor positive, recipient negative',[
 ('Donor negative, recipient negative','This is generally lower risk, subject to other exposures.'),('Donor negative, recipient positive','Reactivation risk exists but primary high-risk mismatch is D+/R−.'),('All combinations have identical risk','Serostatus materially affects prevention planning.')],
 'A nonimmune recipient can acquire donor-derived CMV', '**D+/R− is a major high-risk mismatch.**',['The intensity of immunosuppression further modifies risk.','Serostatus predicts risk but does not diagnose active disease.'], '**Choose a prevention strategy and duration suited to the risk.**',['Prophylaxis or structured preemptive monitoring requires reliable follow-up.','Adjust drug dosing for renal function.'],[['D+/R−','Highest primary-infection risk'],['R+','Reactivation risk'],['D−/R−','Lower baseline risk']],[CM])
q('What is a current prophylaxis option for high-risk D+/R− kidney recipients that may reduce valganciclovir-associated myelotoxicity?', 'Letermovir in an appropriate transplant prevention protocol',[
 ('Letermovir as routine monotherapy for established severe CMV disease','Its major role here is prophylaxis, not standard treatment of established disease.'),('No CMV prevention is needed if leukocytes are low','High-risk recipients still need an effective strategy.'),('Acyclovir at an HSV prophylaxis dose is an equivalent substitute for all CMV risk','That is not an equivalent high-risk CMV prevention regimen.')],
 'Prevention options have expanded', '**Letermovir prophylaxis was noninferior to valganciclovir in a high-risk kidney-transplant trial.**',['The 2025 consensus incorporates this option.','Drug interactions and the lack of HSV/VZV activity require planning.'], '**Distinguish prevention from treatment.**',['Use appropriate herpesvirus prophylaxis when needed.','Follow the center’s duration, interaction and virological monitoring plan.'],[['Letermovir role','CMV prophylaxis'],['Potential advantage','Less myelotoxicity'],['Additional consideration','HSV/VZV coverage and interactions']],[CM])
q('A kidney recipient has severe CMV gastrointestinal disease with unreliable oral absorption. Which treatment is generally preferred initially?', 'Intravenous ganciclovir with renal dose adjustment',[
 ('Oral valganciclovir regardless of absorption or severity','It is suitable for many nonsevere cases but not all severe absorption-limited disease.'),('Letermovir as the usual sole treatment','It is not standard initial therapy for established severe CMV disease.'),('Stop treatment as soon as fever resolves without virological assessment','Duration requires clinical and virological response assessment.')],
 'Treatment route reflects severity and absorption', '**Ganciclovir and valganciclovir are major first-line CMV treatments in appropriate settings.**',['Severe disease or poor absorption favors IV therapy.','Cytopenias and kidney function require monitoring.'], '**Assess response and resistance when appropriate.**',['Ensure correct dosing before assuming resistance.','Use tissue evaluation when organ disease requires confirmation.'],[['Nonsevere/reliable absorption','Often oral valganciclovir'],['Severe/poor absorption','IV ganciclovir'],['Monitoring','Viral, clinical, count and renal response']],[CM],'clinical_scenario')
q('Which prophylactic drug is commonly used to prevent Pneumocystis pneumonia after kidney transplantation?', 'Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole',[
 ('Fluconazole as equivalent Pneumocystis prophylaxis','It does not provide this protection.'),('Valganciclovir as a complete substitute','Its main role is CMV prevention/treatment.'),('No prophylaxis is ever needed after discharge','Risk persists after the immediate surgical admission.')],
 'A familiar antimicrobial prevents an opportunistic pneumonia', '**TMP-SMX is the usual preferred prophylaxis when tolerated.**',['Duration depends on protocol and risk, including rejection treatment.','Hyperkalemia, cytopenias and allergy require monitoring.'], '**Use an appropriate alternative when contraindicated.**',['Reassess prophylaxis after intensified immunosuppression.','Do not assume CMV prophylaxis also covers Pneumocystis.'],[['Target','Pneumocystis'],['Usual drug','TMP-SMX'],['Monitoring','Potassium, counts and tolerance']],[TR,EA])
q('A transplant recipient develops fever, lymphadenopathy and a mass lesion with concern for PTLD. What is the diagnostic priority?', 'Tissue diagnosis with appropriate pathological and viral assessment',[
 ('EBV DNAemia alone definitively establishes every PTLD subtype','Viral load is not a substitute for tissue diagnosis.'),('Increase immunosuppression empirically in all cases','This may worsen lymphoproliferation.'),('A negative EBV blood test excludes all PTLD','EBV-negative PTLD can occur.')],
 'PTLD encompasses more than one biological entity', '**Immunosuppression impairs control of lymphoid proliferation, often but not always EBV-associated.**',['Risk varies with recipient status and treatment exposure.','Extranodal or CNS disease can occur.'], '**Coordinate transplant and hematology management.**',['Reduction of immunosuppression and specific oncological therapy depend on subtype and severity.','Balance graft and malignancy risks explicitly.'],[['Diagnostic anchor','Tissue pathology'],['EBV','Often associated, not universal'],['Care','Transplant plus hematology']],[TR,BE],'clinical_scenario')
q('Heavy proteinuria appears very early after transplantation in a recipient whose original kidney disease was primary FSGS. Which diagnosis deserves urgent consideration?', 'Recurrent primary FSGS',[
 ('Recurrent genetic FSGS is equally inevitable in every mutation','Many kidney-intrinsic genetic forms have a much lower recurrence risk.'),('All early proteinuria is normal and should be ignored','Marked proteinuria needs evaluation.'),('BK nephropathy is the only cause of early proteinuria','The timing and original disease raise a specific recurrence concern.')],
 'Recurrence risk depends on the original disease mechanism', '**Primary circulating-factor-associated FSGS can recur rapidly.**',['Electron microscopy can show early diffuse foot-process effacement.','Adaptive or genetic FSGS has a different recurrence profile.'], '**Confirm the cause and start specialist management promptly.**',['Exclude rejection and other proteinuric lesions.','Original biopsy and genetic history can refine counseling.'],[['Primary FSGS','Can recur early'],['Genetic/adaptive disease','Different recurrence risk'],['Evaluation','Protein quantification and graft assessment']],[TR,EA],'clinical_scenario')
q('Why is medication adherence assessment essential in a recipient with late graft dysfunction and new donor-specific antibodies?', 'Underexposure can promote alloimmunity, but barriers should be identified without blame',[
 ('New DSA proves deliberate nonadherence in every patient','Drug interactions, access problems and biological factors can also contribute.'),('A single normal trough proves perfect long-term exposure','One level cannot reconstruct all prior exposure.'),('Adherence becomes irrelevant after the first year','Late underexposure can remain consequential.')],
 'Treat the exposure problem and its cause', '**Complex regimens, cost, adverse effects and misunderstanding can undermine continuity.**',['Serial levels and medication reconciliation can reveal patterns.','Tissue assessment may be needed to define the graft injury.'], '**Use practical support and shared problem solving.**',['Simplify safely when feasible.','Coordinate refills, education and follow-up rather than relying on admonition alone.'],[['Signal','Late dysfunction/new DSA'],['Assessment','Exposure and practical barriers'],['Response','Support plus injury-specific treatment']],[TR,EA])
q('A transplant recipient asks about live attenuated vaccines during maintenance immunosuppression. Which general principle is correct?', 'They are generally avoided during significant immunosuppression; complete indicated vaccines before transplant when feasible',[
 ('All vaccines are forbidden after transplantation','Inactivated and other nonlive vaccines have important roles.'),('Live vaccines are always safe regardless of immunosuppressive intensity','They can cause vaccine-related disease.'),('Vaccination status does not matter before transplant','Pretransplant review is an important preventive opportunity.')],
 'Vaccine type and immune status determine suitability', '**Nonlive vaccines are generally safer but may produce weaker immune responses.**',['Timing relative to transplantation and rejection therapy affects response.','Selected exceptional live-vaccine situations require specialist protocols.'], '**Use current local transplant vaccination guidance.**',['Review household exposure precautions.','Maintain routine preventive care and cancer screening as part of long-term graft care.'],[['Before transplant','Complete indicated vaccines when feasible'],['After transplant','Nonlive vaccines per guidance'],['Live vaccines','Generally avoid during significant immunosuppression']],[TC,TR])

